      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "RATIONALE",
          "content": "Tongue metastasis from lung cancer is extremely rare, and the prognosis of these patients is rather poor."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "PATIENT CONCERS",
          "content": "A 56-year-old man was found a 4-cm cavity lesion in the left upper lobe, which was initially misdiagnosed as tuberculosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "DIAGNOSES",
          "content": "A case of lung squamous cell carcinoma that metastasized to the base of a patient's tongue."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "UNASSIGNED",
          "Label": "INTERVATIONS",
          "content": "We send the biopsy of the lung and the tongue lesions for gene sequencing."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "OUTCOMES",
          "content": "He received systemic chemotherapy, but continued to have pain at the base of his tongue and died 7 months later."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "LESSONS",
          "content": "From sequencing data, mutations in KRAS proto-oncogene, GTPase (KRAS), phosphatidylinositol-4,5-bisphosphate 3-kinase catalytic subunit alpha (PIK3CA), and tumor protein p53 (TP53) were found in the tumor biopsy of the patient. All of these were indicators of poor prognosis."
        }
      ]},
